WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.935
"Preventing and Treating Sarcoidosis"

00:00:07.146 --> 00:00:10.000
Sarcoidosis is a disease
characterized by granulomas,

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.760
which are collections
of immune cells

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.760
caused by chronic inflammation

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.320
that can form in our lungs,
lymph nodes, on our skin,

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.640
and in other parts of our body.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.520
The occurrence of sarcoidosis
varies greatly around the world,

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.400
from fairly low levels
in countries like South Korea,

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:25.760
Taiwan, and Japan

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:28.000
to up to a hundred times
higher levels

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.720
in Sweden and Canada.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.080
Race also seems to matter,

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:35.280
with Black populations experiencing
much higher rates of disease

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.400
and higher rates of death
at younger ages.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.560
It's possible Black people
may be at greater risk

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.400
of disease
and disease severity,

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:43.680
but there are
also long-standing

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.520
systemic health
and social inequities

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:47.600
that limit access
to quality care.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.640
The routine way
to treat sarcoidosis

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.040
is with immune-suppressing
corticosteroid drugs

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.120
to manage the inflammation.

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:57.040
Drugs come with their own
limitations, though,

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:58.480
including steroid resistance,

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.320
no response to the medications,

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:03.120
and a long list
of potential side effects.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.080
Could nutrition help
in sarcoidosis treatment?

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:07.200
Possibly.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.400
Certain components in food

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.600
may have anti-inflammatory
effects in lung diseases,

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:13.920
so, maybe sarcoidosis patients
should be encouraged

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.200
to eat antioxidant
and polyphenol-rich diets,

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.960
but there have yet to be
interventional trials

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.560
to put it to the test.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:21.920
Are there any steps
we can take

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.160
to potentially avoid
this disease?

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.480
Its cause is unknown.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:28.640
Sarcoidosis can cluster
in families,

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:29.920
but maybe that's just because

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.400
of a shared household
environment.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.760
Although some gene variants
may increase susceptibility,

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.360
genes alone are insufficient
to cause sarcoidosis.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:42.240
Something is driving
the disease process.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.640
Something is driving
the inflammation.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.800
We may be closer to solving
one part of the mystery

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.160
of sarcoidosis.

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:51.360
Recent advances in molecular
and immunological methods

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.200
have produced
a more rigorous look

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:54.960
at the drivers of the disease,

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.000
indicating that mycobacteria
are likely involved

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.520
in at least a subset
of sarcoidosis cases.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.040
Mycobacteria like

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.600
Mycobacterium avium
paratuberculosis,

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.320
or MAP, M-A-P,
is a zoonotic pathogen,

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.120
meaning it can go
from animals to humans.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.600
We previously covered
the potential link between MAP

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:17.120
and other autoimmune conditions,

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:18.880
like type 1 diabetes,

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:21.120
and how dairy products
are a major exposure source

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.600
in our diets to MAP.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.520
Viable MAP bacteria
are still being found

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:26.800
in pasteurized milk,

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:29.440
despite efforts to extend
the pasteurization processes

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:31.040
to prevent this.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:32.640
It's also found in goat milk

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.480
and various types
of cheeses:

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.640
Swiss, cheddar, sheep,
and goat cheeses,

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.240
along with softer cheeses
like mozzarella and burrata.

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:41.840
Unfortunately,
this is a global issue

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.480
in countries raising cows
in intensive systems,

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:46.960
with among the highest rates
in the United States,

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.120
where 68 to 91 percent
of dairy herds are infected.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.800
In this 2015 review,

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.560
two studies looked
for MAP bacteria

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:56.960
and found none
in either sarcoidosis patients

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.880
or control subjects.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.680
And two other studies looked
at evidence of MAP exposure

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.480
and found between 7
and 17 times the odds

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:05.840
of reacting to MAP,

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.800
but neither reached
statistical significance.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.640
What have we learned
since then?

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.000
In a natural experiment,

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.920
researchers looked at the rates
of sarcoidosis

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:17.600
among those who did or did not
receive the BCG vaccine,

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:19.200
which provides
partial protection

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.800
against mycobacteria
like tuberculosis.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:24.320
And those born during a time

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:26.880
with high
BCG vaccination uptake

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.440
had lower rates of sarcoidosis.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.000
But it's not like it was a
randomized controlled experiment,

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:32.720
so it's not clear

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:35.360
if tuberculosis vaccination
could really protect

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:38.160
from MAP-associated
autoimmune diseases,

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:39.840
which also include
Crohn's disease,

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.120
Hashimoto's thyroiditis,
MS, rheumatoid arthritis,

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:45.200
and lupus.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.600
The strongest evidence would
come from seeing an effect

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:50.960
from treating someone
with antimycobacterial drugs.

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:52.720
That's how Barry Marshall
got the Nobel prize

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.280
for proving that H. pylori
caused stomach inflammation,

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:56.880
by swallowing some,

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.600
then curing himself
with antibiotics.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:03.440
This is the study that excited
the sarcoidosis community.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:04.880
Cardiac sarcoidosis,

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:06.720
meaning sarcoidosis
of the heart,

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.600
resolved with MAP antibiotics.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.760
MAP was first identified
in this patient's blood,

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.560
and after more than a year
of a MAP antibiotic regime,

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:17.520
his sarcoidosis appeared
to disappear,

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:20.560
something that was never
even thought possible.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.120
A pilot study in those
with pulmonary sarcoidosis

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:24.400
also found impressive results

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.480
with antimycobacterial therapy,

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:28.080
with improvements
in lung function

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.080
and quality of life.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:32.960
When the researchers conducted
a larger trial, however,

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:35.920
there was no significant benefit
for lung function

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:37.440
despite a significant reduction

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:40.800
in mycobacterial
immune responses.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:42.720
So, where does that leave us?

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.880
Mycobacterial avium
paratuberculosis

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:47.520
in the food supply
is a public health issue.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:49.440
"To reduce human exposure
to MAP

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:51.600
via consumption of dairy
and meat products,

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:53.600
more studies are needed
for estimating"

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:55.920
how much MAP
is in the milk, meat,

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:57.920
and feces of these animals

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:01.360
and how much fecal contamination
is in the milk and meat,

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:04.991
to figure out what we
need to do to kill it.

